body:
Metadata services currently lack database persistence support. This redesigns the spec-layer protocols to enable datasource-backed metadata storage, where switching backends (InMemory → PostgreSQL → MySQL) requires only a driver plugin swap with zero Metadata layer changes.

### MetadataRecordSchema — ADR-0002 fields

- `tenantId` — multi-tenant isolation
- `version` (default 1) — optimistic concurrency
- `checksum` — change detection
- `source` (enum: `filesystem | database | api | migration`) — origin marker
- `tags` — classification/filtering

### MetadataManagerConfigSchema — datasource support

Redesigned in both `system/` and `kernel/` layers:

```typescript
MetadataManagerConfigSchema = z.object({
  datasource: z.string().optional(),          // references DatasourceSchema.name, resolved at runtime via driver.{name}
  tableName: z.string().default('sys_metadata'),
  fallback: z.enum(['filesystem', 'memory', 'none']).default('none'),
  rootDir: z.string().optional(),
  // ...
});
```

Removed obsolete fields: `loaders`, `basePath`, `watchOptions` (as object).

### IMetadataService — fully async

Breaking change. All methods now return Promises. `register` takes explicit `(type, name, data)` signature. Added `exists()` and `listNames()`.

```typescript
interface IMetadataService {
  register(type: string, name: string, data: unknown): Promise<void>;
  get(type: string, name: string): Promise<unknown | undefined>;
  list(type: string): Promise<unknown[]>;
  exists(type: string, name: string): Promise<boolean>;
  listNames(type: string): Promise<string[]>;
  // ...
}
```

### MetadataLoaderContractSchema — `datasource:` protocol

Protocol field changed from free string to enum: `'file:' | 'http:' | 's3:' | 'datasource:'`. Consistent colon-suffix convention across both system and kernel layers.

### Consuming code updated

- `MetadataFacade` — async implementation with `exists()`, `listNames()`
- `dev-plugin` metadata stub — async interface alignment

### Test results

90 metadata-specific tests (up from 70), 4859 total tests across 184 files — zero regressions.

